WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:04.350
[MUSIC]

00:00:04.350 --> 00:00:05.497
Ten years ago,

00:00:05.497 --> 00:00:10.360
I worked with my friend Luke on
the hot dusty plains of West Texas.

00:00:11.490 --> 00:00:14.190
I recently reconnected with Luke, and

00:00:14.190 --> 00:00:17.100
when I did he shared with
me an unsettling story.

00:00:18.830 --> 00:00:20.470
When Luke was in college and

00:00:20.470 --> 00:00:24.440
recently married,
he came across an abandoned hunting riffle

00:00:25.670 --> 00:00:28.790
which he dutifully took down to
the local police station to turn in.

00:00:30.840 --> 00:00:35.400
While he was there, he was told that
the riffle had been reported missing and

00:00:35.400 --> 00:00:39.120
he was under arrest for possession of
a stolen firearm, a serious felony.

00:00:41.630 --> 00:00:48.430
Luke spent the next six days In jail on
charges that were ultimately dropped.

00:00:50.530 --> 00:00:54.800
For the first three days his wife had no
idea where he was because she had an out

00:00:54.800 --> 00:01:00.480
of state cell phone number and the jail
wouldn't allow long distance calls.

00:01:00.480 --> 00:01:04.989
What was it that kept Luke in jail not
just over night but, for six days?

00:01:06.610 --> 00:01:10.540
It was bail set at $100,000.

00:01:10.540 --> 00:01:13.720
Now if your college jobs were

00:01:13.720 --> 00:01:17.692
all like mine which at one point included
campus bookstore return specialist.

00:01:17.692 --> 00:01:20.470
&gt;&gt; [LAUGH]
&gt;&gt; $100,000 was just

00:01:20.470 --> 00:01:23.660
way beyond out of reach and
it was for Luke too.

00:01:25.410 --> 00:01:26.840
But bail is rigid.

00:01:28.260 --> 00:01:29.470
It isn't means tested.

00:01:30.580 --> 00:01:34.570
It's an old system that needs reform
to better align with our values.

00:01:37.740 --> 00:01:43.080
We live in a society that values the
promise of liberty and justice for all.

00:01:44.408 --> 00:01:49.960
But our pre-trial justice system is only
delivering liberty and justice for some.

00:01:52.560 --> 00:01:54.660
So today, can we do three things together?

00:01:55.680 --> 00:01:58.830
First, let's take a walk into
this pretrial justice system.

00:01:58.830 --> 00:02:03.670
Something that you may have never thought
about and until recently I hadn't either.

00:02:04.920 --> 00:02:07.810
Second, let's consider if it matters
whether someone spends the time

00:02:07.810 --> 00:02:10.410
between their arrest and
their trial in jail.

00:02:10.410 --> 00:02:12.050
And third, let's look at ways that you and

00:02:12.050 --> 00:02:13.980
I will interact with
the system going forward.

00:02:15.150 --> 00:02:19.010
Before we get going, I just feel
an obligation to acknowledge that I am not

00:02:19.010 --> 00:02:21.930
the typical person to
address a topic like this.

00:02:21.930 --> 00:02:24.050
I've had a lucky upbringing.

00:02:24.050 --> 00:02:27.000
Nobody in my family spent time in jail,
but

00:02:27.000 --> 00:02:31.920
it makes me angry to think about Luke and
his wife separated for six days.

00:02:31.920 --> 00:02:38.060
And it isn't just Luke, every night in
the US, 700,000 people sit in local jails.

00:02:40.255 --> 00:02:47.510
60% of them are there just waiting for
their trial.

00:02:47.510 --> 00:02:49.150
So to walk into this system.

00:02:49.150 --> 00:02:52.550
After a rest,
everyone will face one three doors.

00:02:52.550 --> 00:02:57.230
So, door number one, release.

00:02:57.230 --> 00:03:01.850
This is for people who are low risk, not
too much of a danger to the community and

00:03:01.850 --> 00:03:04.360
are likely to show up to
all of their court dates.

00:03:04.360 --> 00:03:06.430
They're free to go until
the time of their trial.

00:03:10.565 --> 00:03:12.670
Door number two, detention.

00:03:14.070 --> 00:03:15.950
This is where people who are high risk.

00:03:15.950 --> 00:03:19.120
They may not be likely to show up
to all of their court dates or

00:03:19.120 --> 00:03:22.790
they may have been charged with
a particularly egregious offense and so

00:03:22.790 --> 00:03:24.690
they'll stay in jail until
the time of the trial.

00:03:27.940 --> 00:03:28.620
Door number three.

00:03:29.920 --> 00:03:30.420
Bail.

00:03:31.440 --> 00:03:33.840
This is the door that most
people have to walk through.

00:03:35.320 --> 00:03:38.440
Bail requires that someone
deposited money with the court

00:03:38.440 --> 00:03:42.370
that they will only get back even when
they show up for all of their court dates.

00:03:42.370 --> 00:03:44.900
And if you think about it, that's
kind of a good system and principle.

00:03:44.900 --> 00:03:48.200
I mean, we want some sort of incentive for
people to show up for court.

00:03:49.200 --> 00:03:50.190
But when you dig into the data,

00:03:50.190 --> 00:03:52.720
it's clear this is a tricky
door to have to walk through.

00:03:52.720 --> 00:03:55.520
You see in San Jose two years ago,

00:03:55.520 --> 00:04:00.665
the average bail amount was $27,000.

00:04:00.665 --> 00:04:06.250
$27,000 is enough to buy a brand new
Toyota Camry with leather seats or

00:04:06.250 --> 00:04:09.590
make a down payment on a house
anywhere outside of the bay area.

00:04:09.590 --> 00:04:14.540
[LAUGH] This is like a lot of money.

00:04:14.540 --> 00:04:19.050
And to me this is compelling evidence
that our pre-trial justice system

00:04:19.050 --> 00:04:22.220
keeps people in jail simply based
on who can and who cannot pay.

00:04:22.220 --> 00:04:26.080
So on the one hand you have people with
means, who can pay their way out of jail

00:04:26.080 --> 00:04:30.760
regardless of their risk of society, and
on the other hand you have people like

00:04:30.760 --> 00:04:35.570
Luke, people who are stuck behind
bars simply because they can't pay.

00:04:37.650 --> 00:04:40.570
Now, to topic number two,
it doesn't matter if someone spends

00:04:40.570 --> 00:04:43.570
this time between arrest and
trial, in jail or not.

00:04:45.210 --> 00:04:49.815
If someone is unable to pay bill, they can
wait for weeks or months for that trial.

00:04:51.060 --> 00:04:54.210
And over the course of that time,
they're at risk of losing a job.

00:04:55.310 --> 00:04:59.800
A relationship with a significant
other and if and when they do get out,

00:04:59.800 --> 00:05:02.440
it is that much harder to put
food on the table or pay rent.

00:05:04.450 --> 00:05:06.570
Now, it isn't just this
sort of run of the mill,

00:05:06.570 --> 00:05:10.700
everyday life stuff that gets,
that changes.

00:05:10.700 --> 00:05:13.990
Brooklyn defender services looked at
two groups of people that they serve in

00:05:13.990 --> 00:05:15.140
New York City.

00:05:15.140 --> 00:05:15.800
Group number one,

00:05:15.800 --> 00:05:19.590
these are folks who stayed in jail between
the time of their arrest and trial.

00:05:19.590 --> 00:05:21.540
And group number two,
these are people who got out.

00:05:21.540 --> 00:05:23.480
You know what they found.

00:05:25.140 --> 00:05:28.410
Group number one was five times

00:05:28.410 --> 00:05:31.150
more likely to end up with
a criminal conviction.

00:05:31.150 --> 00:05:35.560
Five times.
And you know what contributes to this.

00:05:35.560 --> 00:05:38.760
It's the fact that when you're in jail
it's hard to meet with your lawyer.

00:05:40.330 --> 00:05:41.760
It's hard to build your case.

00:05:43.080 --> 00:05:46.700
And some people will plead guilty
simply to move the process along.

00:05:48.070 --> 00:05:52.360
But that criminal conviction follows them
when they apply for jobs in the future or

00:05:52.360 --> 00:05:53.380
try to rent an apartment.

00:05:54.940 --> 00:05:59.400
And so the bottom line here is
that getting out really matters.

00:06:02.250 --> 00:06:06.180
To illustrate this, I'd like to share
the story of Kalief Browder, and

00:06:06.180 --> 00:06:09.220
some of you maybe
familiar his tragic tale.

00:06:09.220 --> 00:06:12.490
Kalief was a 16 year old living in
New York City when he was arrested for

00:06:12.490 --> 00:06:13.630
allegedly stealing a backpack.

00:06:14.820 --> 00:06:17.841
His bail was set at only $3000.

00:06:17.841 --> 00:06:19.850
But he and
his family couldn't afford that.

00:06:22.320 --> 00:06:25.870
Calif spent the next three
years on Rikers Island,

00:06:25.870 --> 00:06:28.050
a notorious jail facility
in New York in New York.

00:06:30.160 --> 00:06:31.670
At the end of those three years.

00:06:31.670 --> 00:06:34.000
His charges were dropped.

00:06:34.000 --> 00:06:35.101
And he wasn't convicted of anything.

00:06:38.123 --> 00:06:43.100
And sadly just a couple years after
his release, Kalief committed suicide.

00:06:43.100 --> 00:06:46.050
And many point to the mental and

00:06:46.050 --> 00:06:50.770
emotional trauma of his time in jail
as a major contributing factor.

00:06:50.770 --> 00:06:52.060
So how does this happen.

00:06:52.060 --> 00:06:53.445
I mean this is insane.

00:06:53.445 --> 00:06:58.390
$3,000 cost him 3 years and his life.

00:06:58.390 --> 00:07:02.450
Kalief didn't have his liberty and
as a result he lost his shot at justice.

00:07:03.690 --> 00:07:08.692
So again, the bottom line here is
the getting out really matters.

00:07:12.544 --> 00:07:16.229
How are you and I going to interact
with this system going forward?

00:07:17.350 --> 00:07:21.980
Now, we may never have to walk
through anyone of these three doors.

00:07:23.630 --> 00:07:27.140
But you're going to read articles
about this topic online and

00:07:27.140 --> 00:07:30.370
you'll be asked to vote on criminal
justice reform issues in your home state.

00:07:31.830 --> 00:07:36.440
I am not the only person talking about
this, John Oliver did a spot on bail.

00:07:36.440 --> 00:07:39.662
The New York Times and
NPR are writing about it.

00:07:39.662 --> 00:07:43.400
And Jay-Z actually just produced a mini
series on the Kalief Browder story.

00:07:43.400 --> 00:07:47.800
So read these articles and get informed
because you will be asked to weigh in.

00:07:47.800 --> 00:07:53.080
I'd like to share two examples of this.

00:07:53.080 --> 00:07:56.640
In 2014, New Jersey passed
a ballot measure that did away

00:07:56.640 --> 00:07:58.030
with their old money bail system.

00:07:59.080 --> 00:08:01.720
And he replaced it with a risk algorithm.

00:08:01.720 --> 00:08:07.060
What this does is, it scores every
defendant on a scale from one to six.

00:08:07.060 --> 00:08:10.140
It's based on their likelihood
of not showing up to court and

00:08:10.140 --> 00:08:11.740
the risk to the community.

00:08:11.740 --> 00:08:14.960
This score based on their actual
risk is what determines whether they

00:08:14.960 --> 00:08:16.600
stay in jail or not.

00:08:16.600 --> 00:08:18.600
It's not their ability to pay.

00:08:18.600 --> 00:08:21.980
And while these algorithms
are not perfect by any means

00:08:21.980 --> 00:08:24.500
they're an important step
forward in the right direction.

00:08:26.480 --> 00:08:29.650
A little bit closer to home, there's
actually a bill working its way through

00:08:29.650 --> 00:08:32.940
the California legislature currently
that would have similar effects.

00:08:32.940 --> 00:08:35.730
So this is relevant for us right now.

00:08:35.730 --> 00:08:37.380
It would shift people
from door number three,

00:08:37.380 --> 00:08:40.300
bail through door number 1, release.

00:08:42.660 --> 00:08:46.650
Now, for the last year, I've been working
with a team of students here in Stanford

00:08:46.650 --> 00:08:49.090
for a business based
solution to this problem.

00:08:50.100 --> 00:08:54.930
And if this is at all interesting to you,
I'm all ears please come talk to me.

00:08:54.930 --> 00:08:57.683
Small interested folks like yourselves,
are critical for

00:08:57.683 --> 00:08:59.180
coming up with a better system.

00:09:01.363 --> 00:09:04.980
So I want you to leave here
today knowing that this matters.

00:09:06.300 --> 00:09:07.635
This matters to the Lukes.

00:09:09.110 --> 00:09:11.660
It matters to the Kaliefs.

00:09:11.660 --> 00:09:15.748
And it matters to thousands of others
who are trapped in our pre-trial

00:09:15.748 --> 00:09:17.380
justice system.

00:09:17.380 --> 00:09:18.990
So let's renew a commitment to our values.

00:09:20.180 --> 00:09:25.330
And let's take an important step forward
in delivering on the promise of liberty

00:09:25.330 --> 00:09:32.242
and justice, not just for some,
but liberty and justice for all.

00:09:32.242 --> 00:09:36.136
&gt;&gt; [APPLAUSE]

00:09:36.136 --> 00:09:40.079
[MUSIC]

